@startuml proto-servo-frame
' ===========================================================================
' proto-servo-frame.puml - (b) the servo "frame": a pulse, a gap, and the table
' that produces them.
'
' A servo frame is the simplest structure in this repository and the easiest to
' get subtly wrong, because there is nothing to decode: no header, no length, no
' checksum, no acknowledge. The only quantities are a high time and a low time,
' and the ONLY relationship a real servo depends on is that their sum is
' 20 ms.
' ===========================================================================

title Servo PWM - the frame and the two tables\n<font size=12>the frame is a SLOT: 20 ms minus the pulse, whatever the pulse is</font>

left to right direction
hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 12
}
skinparam rectangle<<pulse>> {
  BackgroundColor #FFE9B0
  BorderColor #C09000
}
skinparam rectangle<<gap>> {
  BackgroundColor #DCE6F5
  BorderColor #5577AA
}
skinparam rectangle<<one>> {
  BackgroundColor #FFF4D6
  BorderColor #C8B800
}
skinparam rectangle<<short>> {
  BackgroundColor #E8F0E8
  BorderColor #557755
}

rectangle "PULSE\n<b>1.000 ms</b>\n\nthe datasheet's\nMINIMUM position\n0 degrees\n\ndmem[0] = 97" as P0 <<pulse>>
rectangle "GAP\n<b>19.000 ms</b>\n\nFULL 20 ms SLOT\nthe frame rate is\nmeasured here\n\ndmem[5] = 229" as G0 <<one>>
rectangle "PULSE\n<b>1.500 ms</b>\n\ncentre\n\ndmem[1] = 145" as P1 <<pulse>>
rectangle "GAP\n<b>18.500 ms</b>\n\nFULL 20 ms SLOT\nmeasured against G0:\n<b>19 999.95 us = 50.000 Hz</b>\n\ndmem[6] = 223" as G1 <<one>>
rectangle "PULSE\n<b>1.750 ms</b>\n\ndmem[2] = 169" as P2 <<pulse>>
rectangle "GAP\n<b>2.500 ms</b>\n\na SHORT sweep gap,\nnot a frame\n\ndmem[7] = 31" as G2 <<short>>
rectangle "PULSE\n<b>1.250 ms</b>\n\ndmem[3] = 121" as P3 <<pulse>>
rectangle "GAP\n<b>2.500 ms</b>\n\ndmem[8] = 31" as G3 <<short>>
rectangle "PULSE\n<b>2.000 ms</b>\n\nthe datasheet's\nMAXIMUM position\nfull travel\n\ndmem[4] = 193" as P4 <<pulse>>
rectangle "GAP\n<b>2.500 ms</b>\n\ndmem[9] = 31" as G4 <<short>>
rectangle "dmem[10] = 5\n\nthe sweep is done.\nThe firmware parks with\nthe line LOW." as END

P0 -[#3A7]-> G0 : rise 0
G0 -[#3A7]-> P1 : rise 1, <b>19 999.95 us later</b>
P1 -[#3A7]-> G1
G1 -[#3A7]-> P2 : rise 2, the short gap begins
P2 -[#3A7]-> G2
G2 -[#3A7]-> P3 : rise 3
P3 -[#3A7]-> G3
G3 -[#3A7]-> P4 : rise 4
P4 -[#3A7]-> G4
G4 -[#3A7]-> END
END -[#3A7,dashed]-> P0 : and the firmware loops

note bottom of P0
  <b>Why the order is 1.0, 1.5, 1.75, 1.25, 2.0 and not monotonic.</b>

  A sweep that only grows can be satisfied by a
  firmware that emits the right widths in the
  wrong order, and a testbench that sorts its
  measurements before checking them cannot tell
  the difference. Emitting a NON-monotonic order
  makes the order a checkable property: width i
  is compared against position i, and the mutation
  case sv-sweep-order swaps two entries to prove it.
end note

note top of G1
  <b>Why the frame period is measured across two different pulse widths.</b>

  1000 us then 1500 us, rise to rise. That is the
  measurement that tells a SLOT from a DELAY: a
  fixed gap after each pulse gives 20.5 ms here
  and 21.0 ms at the next position, so both the
  frame rate and the sweep would be wrong in ways
  no per-pulse width check can see.

  This is the act's only tolerance-shaped number
  and it is the one that is stated as a band
  (19 900 .. 20 100 us) rather than an equality,
  because a frame period is two measured widths
  added together and the program's is a counted
  instruction count, not a calibrated one - the
  error is in the two delay tables' fitting, which
  is bounded at 0.25 us each by construction.
end note

note bottom of G2
  <b>Why three of the five slots are short, and it is stated.</b>

  Positions 0 and 1 are a full 20 ms apart so the
  frame rate can be measured. Positions 2..4 use a
  2.5 ms gap - a fifth - which saves 45 ms of
  simulated time in the longest testbench in the
  repository.

  The trade costs nothing that is claimed: the
  50 Hz claim rests on the measured frame period,
  and the sweep claim rests on five measured
  pulse WIDTHS, which is all a width check needs.
  The TB still checks that each short gap exceeds
  1 000 us, and it PRINTS them as "a short sweep
  gap, not a frame" rather than reporting them as
  slots.
end note

legend bottom
  |= position |= pulse | gap | total slot | pulse n1 | gap n1 |=
  | 0 - 0 degrees    | 1 000 us | 19 000 us | <b>20 000 us (full)</b> |  97 | 229 |
  | 1 - centre        | 1 500 us | 18 500 us | <b>20 000 us (full)</b> | 145 | 223 |
  | 2                 | 1 750 us |  2 500 us | 4 250 us (short) | 169 |  31 |
  | 3                 | 1 250 us |  2 500 us | 3 750 us (short) | 121 |  31 |
  | 4 - full travel   | 2 000 us |  2 500 us | 4 500 us (short) | 193 |  31 |

  The n1 column is the OUTER counter of the delay
  routine. For a chosen (n2,n3) the outer counter
  that lands nearest a target is a DIVISION, and
  choosing (n2,n3) to keep every entry inside 255
  is a small search. Both tables are the result of
  that, not of trial and error.

  | pair | used for | one outer step | quantisation |
  | (2, 152)  | pulses | (4*152 + 11) = 619 clocks = 10.3 us | 10.3 us |
  | (11, 123) | gaps   | (10 + 10*499) = 4 655 clocks = 77.6 us | 77.6 us |

  <b>Why the numbers are this close is the point, and the accuracy is not.</b>
  A servo's 50 Hz is a nominal with a wide tolerance; the real requirement is
  "not 45 Hz and not 58 Hz". The point is WHY: the delay is a DETERMINISTIC
  INSTRUCTION COUNT, not a calibrated loop, so there is no oscillator to drift
  and no phase to lose. Change the clock and every number in the table scales
  with it, visibly, in the source.
end legend

@enduml
